# =============================================================================
# LN KICKS — Production Environment Variables Template
# =============================================================================
#
# AUTO-GENERATED from a full audit of every `process.env.*` and `env("*")`
# reference in the codebase. This file lists ONLY variables actually read by
# the application code — nothing invented, nothing aspirational.
#
# Audit method:
#   grep -rEoh "process\.env\.[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*" \
#     --include="*.ts" --include="*.tsx" --include="*.js" \
#     --exclude-dir=node_modules --exclude-dir=.next \
#     --exclude-dir=scripts --exclude-dir=docs --exclude-dir=prototypes
#
# Combined with Prisma schema env("...") references.
#
# Result — the codebase reads EXACTLY these env vars:
#
#   USER-SET (configure in cPanel → Setup Node.js App → Environment variables):
#     DATABASE_URL                  REQUIRED  — Prisma datasource.url + app.js boot check
#     DIRECT_URL                    REQUIRED  — Prisma datasource.directUrl (migrations)
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL          RECOMMENDED — app.js startup diagnostics
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_WHATSAPP_NUMBER   RECOMMENDED — app.js startup diagnostics
#
#   AUTO-SET (by Next.js / LiteSpeed / OS — DO NOT configure manually):
#     NODE_ENV          — set by Next.js (`next build` → "production")
#     PORT              — set by cPanel/Passenger when not using LSNODE_SOCKET
#     HOSTNAME          — defaults to "0.0.0.0" in app.js
#     LSNODE_SOCKET     — set by LiteSpeed LSAPI (UNIX socket path)
#     HOME              — set by the OS ($HOME)
#
# THAT'S IT. There are no other env vars consumed by the code. Any vars you
# may have seen in older versions of this file (JWT_SECRET, SMTP_*, STRIPE_*,
# RAZORPAY_*, CLOUDINARY_*, MYSQL_*, CORS_ORIGIN, etc.) were ASPIRATIONAL —
# the code does not read them. They have been removed to avoid confusion.
#
# =============================================================================
# WHERE TO SET THESE
# =============================================================================
#
# cPanel → Software → Setup Node.js App → Edit LNKICKS →
# "Environment variables" section → Add each var.
#
# These are written to ~/nodevenv/LNKICKS/22/etc/envvars and read by app.js
# at startup (LiteSpeed/lsnode does NOT source bin/activate, so app.js
# parses etc/envvars itself).
#
# The .cpanel.yml deploy script also writes a .env file in the app root from
# these same envvars as a fallback. Either path works.
#
# =============================================================================
# SECURITY
# =============================================================================
#
# This file is safe to commit — it contains only placeholder values.
# NEVER commit a filled-in .env file. The .gitignore excludes .env* files
# (except this .example file).
#
# =============================================================================

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED — app will REFUSE TO START without these (app.js exits with code 1)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# PostgreSQL connection string (pooled — used by the app at runtime).
# Get this from your Postgres provider's dashboard (Neon / Supabase / Railway /
# Render / self-hosted). MUST include ?sslmode=require for managed Postgres.
# If using Neon's pooler endpoint (-pooler), also append &pgbouncer=true.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/dbname?sslmode=require

# PostgreSQL direct connection (bypasses pooler — used by Prisma CLI for
# migrations and `prisma db pull`). MUST include ?sslmode=require.
# On Neon: this is the NON-pooler hostname (no -pooler suffix).
DIRECT_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/dbname?sslmode=require

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RECOMMENDED — app starts without these, but logs show ❌ at boot if missing
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Public site URL (no trailing slash). Used in app.js startup diagnostics.
# Currently NOT consumed by app code for behavior (metadata uses hardcoded
# values), but set this so startup logs are clean and future code can read it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://your-production-domain.com

# WhatsApp business number (country code + number, no +, no spaces).
# Used in app.js startup diagnostics.
# NOTE: The actual click-to-chat link in app/help-support/page.tsx currently
# uses a hardcoded number (918881286267). Setting this env var does NOT
# change that link — it only makes the startup diagnostic log show ✅.
NEXT_PUBLIC_WHATSAPP_NUMBER=918881286267

# =============================================================================
# END OF FILE — no other env vars are read by the application code.
# =============================================================================
